## 3. Positive-Mass Stationarity And The Correct Coupling Type

Let

\[
 Q(H,d;m)=mH+i(Hd+d^\dagger H),\qquad H>0,\quad m>0. \tag{3}
\]

The matrix `Hd+d^dagger H` is Hermitian. Therefore

\[
 \operatorname{Herm}Q=mH>0.                          \tag{4}
\]

If `Q psi=0`, then the real part of `psi^dagger Q psi` is
`m psi^dagger H psi`, which vanishes only for `psi=0`. Thus `Q` is invertible.
At a zero-source stationary point of

\[
 S_{\rm DK}=\bar\psi Q(h)\psi,                      \tag{5}
\]

one has `psi*=0` and `bar psi*=0`. Consequently

\[
 {\partial^2S_{\rm DK}\over\partial h_A\partial\bar\psi}
 \bigg|_\star=(\partial_AQ)\psi_\star=0,            \tag{6}
\]

even when the genuine three-point vertex `partial_A Q` is nonzero.

This proof is stronger than a sampled rank. The reconstructed scout fixture is
also checked directly:

| diagnostic | exact result |
|---|---:|
| cover dimension | 32 |
| antiperiodic section dimension | 16 |
| rank `Q_AP` at `m=2/7` | 16 |
| determinant | nonzero; numerator/denominator have 143/142 decimal digits |
| declared Hodge-amplitude vertex rank | 16 |
| zero-background mixed Hessian | zero |

The amplitude deformation `H -> (1+s)H` is used only as a nonzero type control:
its vertex is `partial_s Q=Q`. It is not claimed to be the selected metric
coupling. Because `Q` is invertible, an explicitly sourced stationary equation
`Q psi=J` can have nonzero mixed response. That demonstrates the sourced escape
without deriving the source `J`.

A changed carrier also escapes: on the periodic massless Block-106 complex,
`i(d+d^dagger)` has rank 12 and nullity four. This does not refute (3)--(6);
it drops both the positive-mass and antiperiodic-acyclic conditions.

The same typing warning applies to a marked Gaussian. Its classical saddle can
be zero while

\[
 {d\over ds}\det(Q+sE)^{-1}\bigg|_{s=0}
 =-\det(Q)^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}(Q^{-1}E)             \tag{7}
\]

is nonzero. Classical vacuum cross-Hessian visibility is not necessary for a
source derivative or marked insertion to be Record-visible.

## 4. Total Ward Identity, Not An Isolated Cross-Block Null

For the correctly typed vertex (1), Hermitian reversal is

\[
 V_A(-q,k+q)=V_A(q,k)^\dagger.                       \tag{8}
\]

The total first-order Ward equation has the form

\[
 \Gamma_g(q)^T V(q,k)
 +Q(k+q)L(q,k)+\bar L(q,k)Q(k)=0.                   \tag{9}
\]

The last two terms are matter recoil. Deleting them and demanding that the
cross block be separately gauge-null can manufacture source darkness.

More generally, invariance gives

\[
 J_A R_I^A=0,
 \qquad H_{BA}R_I^A=-J_A\partial_B R_I^A.           \tag{10}
\]

Only at a stationary point of the **full** joint action, including the source
and Record variables, may one infer `H R=0`. This is why stationarity precedes
all Hessian rank and gauge-null claims in the corrected tournament.

## 5. Exact Gravity And Constraint Coefficient Classification

The runner next forgets the inherited coefficient values and solves them.
This classification uses the isotropic second-order tensor ansatz, which is
stronger than proper-cubic symmetry and therefore narrower than the eventual
finite-Laurent tournament.

For symmetric spatial tensors,

\[
 (G\pi)_{ij}=\pi_{ij}+b\delta_{ij}\operatorname{tr}\pi, \tag{11}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
 (Ph)_{ij}={}&c_1p^2h_{ij}+c_2p_ip_j\operatorname{tr}h
 +c_3(p_ip^kh_{kj}+p_jp^kh_{ki})\\
 &+c_4\delta_{ij}p^2\operatorname{tr}h
 +c_5\delta_{ij}p^kp^lh_{kl},                       \tag{12}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
 Ch=(p^2\delta^{ij}+d_2p^ip^j)h_{ij},               \tag{13}
\]

\[
 (M\pi)_i=i(-2p^j\pi_{ij}-f_2p_i\operatorname{tr}\pi), \tag{14}
\]

\[
 (S\beta)_{ij}=i[p_i\beta_j+p_j\beta_i
 +f_2\delta_{ij}(p\cdot\beta)].                    \tag{15}
\]

The normalizations in (11), (13)--(15) fix the kinetic, Hamiltonian-constraint,
and shift-generator units. The overall potential scale `c1` remains free.

Coefficient matching is exact in symbolic `(p_x,p_y,p_z)`. The runner imposes

\[
 P=P^T,\quad G=G^T,\quad PS=0,\quad MP=0,
 \quad CS=0,\quad MC^\dagger=0,                    \tag{16}
\]

\[
 CG=-\frac12(ip)^TM.                                \tag{17}
\]

Nineteen distinct polynomial coefficient equations reduce to the Groebner
basis

\[
\left\{
b+\tfrac12,
c_2-c_1,
c_3+c_1,
c_4+c_1,
c_5-c_1,
d_2+1,
f_2
\right\}.                                           \tag{18}
\]

Hence

\[
 b=-\frac12,\quad d_2=-1,\quad f_2=0,
 \quad(c_2,c_3,c_4,c_5)=c_1(1,-1,-1,1).            \tag{19}
\]

This independently recovers the Block-77/78 DeWitt and Fierz--Pauli maps.
It does **not** select `c1`, prove that isotropy is the unique proper-cubic
ansatz, or identify one Record-faithful physical equivalence class. A
